{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>I also, etc.</b> Meaning: the attribute of mercy will also collaborate with the attribute of strict judgment upon the curses." "<b>I will appoint over you, etc. consumption, etc.</b> HaShem chose these species of curses corresponding to the measure of the wicked, and since their principal transgression is withholding from engagement in Torah, He measured them with that very measure. Now Torah is likened to fire, as it is written (Jeremiah 23:29): ״הֲלֹא כֹה דְבָרִי כָאֵשׁ״." "And know that Torah confers three qualities of goodness upon a person's soul. The first: through the mediation of engagement in it, it is a fire that refines the soul and purifies it so that it need not descend to Gehinnom to be refined, and therefore the fire of Gehinnom has no dominion over them (Ḥagigah 27a), for its fire is greater than the power of Gehinnom's fire, and the ailments of the soul that are in them — the fire of Gehinnom has no dominion; and therefore Gehinnom cries out in distress: ״I have no desire for the righteous״ etc. (Exodus Rabbah 7:4), for they weaken its power and extinguish its fire. The second quality is that it illuminates the eyes, for you must know that all Israel require a great light for their eyes, so that they may benefit from the radiance of the Shekhinah on high. And the Master, in His love for us, gave us the Torah, which is called light, as it is written (Proverbs 6:23): ״וְתוֹרָה אוֹר״, so that through its mediation the power of the light of our eyes grows great — for according to the greatness of the light in one's eyes, so is the apprehension attained in gazing toward Elohim; and to all who were not illuminated through Torah, the prophet of HaShem calls them (Isaiah 42:7): ״וְהַעִוְרִים הַבִּיטוּ לִרְאוֹת״. And the third quality is that words of Torah gladden the heart, as it is written (Psalms 19:9): ״פִּקּוּדֵי ה׳ יְשָׁרִים מְשַׂמְּחֵי לֵב״, and it is written (Psalms 97:11): ״וּלְיִשְׁרֵי לֵב שִׂמְחָה״. And corresponding to these three qualities HaShem declared a precise punishment for those who forsake HaShem: corresponding to their refusal to engage in Torah, which is holy fire, HaShem will appoint over them consumption and fever; He also mentioned <b>בֶּהָלָה</b>, which is the opposite of the apprehension attained through engagement in Torah, namely the settling of the intellect and the ordering of the mind; and corresponding to the illumination of the eyes He said <b>מְכַלּוֹת עֵינַיִם</b>; and corresponding to the gladness of the heart He said <b>וְדַאֲבוֹן נָפֶשׁ</b>; and He said <b>וּזְרַעְתֶּם לָרִיק</b> corresponding to the success of this world." "<b>Furthermore,</b> it alludes to the success of the supernal world, after the manner of their statement, of blessed memory (Okl tin 3:12), that the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to bequeath to every single righteous person three hundred and ten worlds. And it is a well-known matter that every gradation of the promised reward for the righteous has a corresponding punishment for the wicked, as it says (Ecclesiastes 7:14): ״גַּם זֶה לְעֻמַּת זֶה עָשָׂה הָאֱלֹהִים״. And just as there are gradations of the three hundred and ten promised to the worthy, corresponding to them are three hundred and ten defilements of suffering for the doers of evil, and it is called קֶרִי רִיק, whose numerical value is three hundred and ten — and that is the statement <b>וּזְרַעְתֶּם לָרִיק</b>: instead of ״to bequeath My beloved what is״, there will be, for the aspect of evil, רִיק, for they correspond to the number in the klippah. And it uses the expression of sowing, for that term is also applied to other activities, after the manner of His statement (Hosea 10:12): ״זִרְעוּ לָכֶם לִצְדָקָה״ etc. And His saying <b>וַאֲכָלֻהוּ אֹיְבֵיכֶם</b> is because the klippot derive nourishment from the deeds of the wicked, and the verse makes known that the klippot are the enemies of man."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {3 "I have rendered the Talmudic tractate reference עוקצין as 'Okl tin' following the mangled form as it appears in the source text; this refers to Masekhet Uktzin."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:02:08.338111325Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}